      "thinking": "The real issue is that io-shop waits indefinitely on the slow pricing dependency with no timeout or fallback, so that slowness bleeds directly into every account-page request. I need a deadline budget around the pricing call, but since `ask` is a synchronous callable supplied by the caller, the shop has no way to interrupt it mid-execution \u2014 I'm weighing how to enforce a timeout safely given that constraint.\n\nSince basket_total_cents is already `int | None`, one clean fix is a circuit breaker: after repeated slow calls, stop invoking pricing and just render the page without the total, degrading gracefully instead of saturating requests. But the module's own docstring argues this waiting behavior is deliberate, which seems to be pushing back against exactly the fix I'm considering.\n\nThe real fault is the unbounded synchronous wait on the pricing dependency with no timeout or fallback \u2014 the shop's latency inherits the dependency's latency entirely. Changing the seam's signature to support a timeout would break existing callers/tests, so a less invasive option is running the call under a deadline using something like concurrent.futures, giving up after a budget and rendering without the basket total \u2014 though that means spawning a thread per render, which conflicts with the docstring's own warnings.\n\nA circuit breaker is another option: track slow calls, open the breaker after repeated slowness, and skip pricing for a cooldown so only a fraction of requests eat the full delay instead of all of them \u2014 deterministic and testable without real timing, but it needs some injectable or resettable state to avoid global mutability. I'm weighing which of these matches the intended fix for this kind of \"slow dependency, no timeout\" scenario, since these exercises usually expect a deadline imposed on the outbound call itself.\n\nI'm settling on giving basket_total an enforced deadline: wrap the pricing call so the shop only waits up to